{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 46, :comments ["<b>And the name of the daughter of Asher was Serach</b> — Since she was alive, Scripture counted her here; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 26:46). Onkelos (Targum Onkelos on Numbers 26:46) translated: \"וְשׁוֹם בַּת אִתַּת אָשֵׁר סָרַח.\" His intention in this was to say that she was a daughter who inherited a portion, and therefore Scripture mentioned her here just as it mentioned the daughters of Zelophehad, and she is included in the category of \"to these the land shall be divided\" (Numbers 26:53). Now, had she been the daughter of Asher himself, she would not have inherited, for he had male sons. Rather, she was the daughter of his wife from another man, who had no son, and whose inheritance therefore went to his daughter. And the meaning of \"and Serach their sister\" (Genesis 46:17) would be that she was the sister of his sons, not his daughter. This is why it says \"the name of the daughter of Asher was Serach\" and does not say \"and the daughter of Asher,\" to say that her name was \"the daughter of Asher\" and she was called Serach. And if she was alive according to Rashi's words, then she is like the daughters of Zelophehad for inheritance; and if she had died — her family took on her account. And according to the plain sense, she had a large family called by her name, and it enters into the category of \"these are the families of the sons of Asher according to those numbered of them\" (Numbers 26:47). But Scripture did not wish to trace them through the woman, to say \"of Serach, the family of the Serachites,\" and so it abbreviated the matter."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:58.806686478Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}